Here's how to save and restore draft text using Window lifecycle events:

## 1. Create a Custom Window Subclass

```csharp
// AppWindow.cs
public class AppWindow : Window
{
    private readonly NoteViewModel _viewModel;

    public AppWindow(Page page, NoteViewModel viewModel) : base(page)
    {
        _viewModel = viewModel;
    }

    protected override void OnStopped()
    {
        base.OnStopped();
        Preferences.Set("draft_text", _viewModel.DraftText);
    }

    protected override void OnResumed()
    {
        base.OnResumed();
        _viewModel.DraftText = Preferences.Get("draft_text", string.Empty);
    }

    protected override void OnDestroying()
    {
        base.OnDestroying();
        // Android back-button can skip Stopped entirely
        Preferences.Set("draft_text", _viewModel.DraftText);
    }
}
```

## 2. Return It from App.CreateWindow

```csharp
// App.xaml.cs
public partial class App : Application
{
    private readonly NoteViewModel _viewModel;

    public App(NoteViewModel viewModel)
    {
        InitializeComponent();
        _viewModel = viewModel;
    }

    protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState? activationState)
        => new AppWindow(new AppShell(), _viewModel);
}
```

## 3. Your ViewModel

```csharp
public partial class NoteViewModel : ObservableObject
{
    [ObservableProperty]
    private string _draftText = string.Empty;
}
```

## Key Points

| Concern | Guidance |
|---------|----------|
| **Why `OnStopped`?** | Fires when the app is fully backgrounded (not visible) |
| **Why also `OnDestroying`?** | On Android, the back button can skip `Stopped` entirely |
| **Why not `OnDeactivated`?** | That fires for transient focus loss (notification shade, dialogs) — too noisy |
| **Keep it fast** | `Preferences` is ideal for small strings; avoid DB writes here to prevent ANR/watchdog kills |

On next launch (cold start), restore the draft in your page's constructor or `OnAppearing`:

```csharp
_viewModel.DraftText = Preferences.Get("draft_text", string.Empty);
```

This covers all scenarios: backgrounding, resuming, and process termination.
